1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hello! Every time you call Cloud in any language, you are really making one kind of HTTP request.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
The Cloud API is a REST interface that speaks JSON in and JSON out. In this lesson, I want you

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
to see that one endpoint clearly and understand why the SDKs are just friendly clients calling it.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Let's ground three words you'll see constantly. First, REST. It just means the Cloud API is a

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:35,000
web service you reach over HTTP. You send a request to a URL with a verb, headers, and a body,

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
and you get a structured response back. Second, JSON. Your request body is JSON,

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
holding the model, the messages, and a few parameters. The response is JSON too,

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
carrying content blocks and metadata, like the stop reason and token usage. Third, the endpoint.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Almost everything you do rides one path, a post to the message's endpoint. Tools, caching, and

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
thinking aren't separate services. They're features of that single call, turned on with fields in the

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
same body. So what actually goes into one of these requests? Think of it as everything Cloud needs

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
to handle a single turn, packed into one JSON body. You name a model so the service knows which

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Cloud you want. You pass a messages array, which is the role-tagged conversation so far,

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
user turns and assistant turns in order, and you set a handful of generation parameters,

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
things like max tokens to cap the length. That's the whole request. What comes back is just as

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
structured. The response is a list of typed content blocks, the actual text or tool calls,

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
wrapped with metadata, like the stop reason and how many tokens you used.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Here's the idea at the heart of this lesson. There are two ways to make that call,

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and they end up in the same place. The first is the raw REST way. You build the HTTP request

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
yourself. You set the headers, turn your data into a JSON string, post it to the endpoint,

21
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
and then parse the JSON that comes back by hand. The second is the SDK way. You write something

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
like client.messages.create, passing typed arguments. Behind that one line, the SDK

23
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
assembles the exact same request, sends it, and hands you back a typed response object.

24
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Same endpoint, same body on the wire. The only difference is how much you write yourself.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
One payoff of the single endpoint design is that you don't relearn a new API for every capability.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
They all ride the same messages call. Want the model to use tools? Tool definitions and the

27
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
tool use loop are just fields you add to the request. Want structured output or extended

28
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
thinking? Those are options on the same call. Want to cache a big chunk of your prompt to save money?

29
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Prompt caching is a control you attach to message blocks. So once you truly understand this one

30
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,000
endpoint and its request shape, every new feature in Anthropic Ships tends to feel familiar. It's

31
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
usually another field on a call you already know how to make. Now, the messages endpoint is the

32
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
workhorse, but it's not completely alone. A few supporting endpoints round out the surface,

33
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and they follow the same REST and JSON style. When you have a large volume of requests that

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
aren't time sensitive, the batches endpoint lets you submit them together and process

35
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
asynchronously at half the standard price. The files endpoint lets you upload documents once and

36
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
reuse them, and the models endpoint lists what's available to your account. And before you spend

37
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
anything, the count tokens endpoint tells you how big a request is so you can budget cost and context

38
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
up front. Same client, same patterns, just different paths. Here's a property that surprises people.

39
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:05,000
The CLAUD API is stateless. The endpoint remembers nothing between your calls. Each request stands

40
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
completely on its own. That means if you're having a multi-turn conversation, you resend the entire

41
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
history every single time, all the prior user and assistant turns in the body of the new request.

42
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
There's no session token, no server-side memory you can lean on to hold the thread for you. When

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
CLAUD replies, it's your job to append that reply back into your messages array before the next call.

44
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
This is why context management is such a big deal for builders. The conversation only exists because

45
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:43,000
you keep resending it, and everything in it counts against your context window. Let's connect the SDK

46
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:50,000
to the REST layer more precisely. First, the SDKs are generated from a single OpenAPI specification.

47
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
That's a machine-readable description of every endpoint. So when a new capability lands in the

48
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
API, it shows up across the Python, TypeScript, and other clients together, not one at a time.

49
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Second, the fields map one-to-one. A JSON field in the API becomes a parameter in the SDK with

50
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
only naming-style differences, like snake case versus camel case. What you read in the API

51
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
reference is what you pass in code. Third, the wire behavior is identical. When you call

52
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
client.messages.create, it's sending the same post to the message's endpoint you'd send by hand.

53
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Nothing extra happens on the network. Why hammer on this one REST call picture? Because it makes

54
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
you a faster builder. When something misbehaves, you can debug at the wire level, log the raw request

55
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,000
and the raw response, and you can see exactly what Claude received and returned. No SDK mystery

56
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
in between. It also means the API documentation applies directly to your SDK code,

57
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
since the shapes match, so you're never hunting for a separate SDK-only reference. And it means

58
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
you're never trapped. If you hit an edge case the SDK doesn't expose cleanly, you can drop down to

59
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:13,000
a raw HTTP call for that one request and lose nothing. The SDK is a convenience layer, not a

60
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:21,000
different product with different powers. Here's the bottom line. The Claude API is one REST interface.

61
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Requests and responses are JSON over HTTP. Almost everything routes through the message's endpoint,

62
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
and the API is stateless. Just remember, an SDK call becomes that exact same REST call

63
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
underneath the covers.

